WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:08.838
I come from one of the most
beautiful places in the world,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.215
Papua New Guinea.

00:00:10.257 --> 00:00:13.385
Papua New Guinea is wonderful
for many reasons.

00:00:13.426 --> 00:00:17.305
Our lush vegetation, our seas,

00:00:17.347 --> 00:00:20.183
islands, lakes and forests.

00:00:20.684 --> 00:00:24.813
Also, our unique and remarkable
cultural diversity.

00:00:25.438 --> 00:00:27.899
Not to forget our people.

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:31.152
And also, not to mention our climate.

00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:34.948
It's basically 27 degrees Celsius,

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:37.200
or 80 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:00:37.242 --> 00:00:39.286
every day, all year.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.831
We get enough rain, but not too much rain.

00:00:42.872 --> 00:00:45.417
We get enough sun, but not too much sun.

00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:47.294
And if you were to live there,

00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:52.215
you don't necessarily have to turn
your heater on in the winter,

00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:56.303
or you don't have to turn
your air conditioning on in the summer.

00:00:56.636 --> 00:00:59.764
So I grew up in this paradise

00:00:59.764 --> 00:01:03.018
until I had to leave home

00:01:03.018 --> 00:01:07.230
to attain a modern education
away from my home.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:12.068
After some years, I returned home

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.987
to be with my people.

00:01:14.779 --> 00:01:16.906
I returned home

00:01:16.948 --> 00:01:19.743
importantly, to be with my people

00:01:19.784 --> 00:01:24.623
to resist against a mining company
that was proposing to extract minerals

00:01:24.664 --> 00:01:26.041
in the Frieda River,

00:01:26.082 --> 00:01:30.670
which is a major tributary
to the Sepik River,

00:01:30.712 --> 00:01:34.007
which is the longest river
in Papua New Guinea.

00:01:35.258 --> 00:01:37.969
The Sepik River is the lifeblood

00:01:38.011 --> 00:01:40.180
and is our heritage.

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:44.517
The people living around the area
are called Sepik.

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:46.269
Interestingly,

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:48.688
that is our identity

00:01:48.688 --> 00:01:53.276
and our existence are intertwined

00:01:53.318 --> 00:01:56.363
with this unhurried body of water,

00:01:56.363 --> 00:01:57.781
this winding river.

00:01:58.823 --> 00:02:04.663
The Sepik is one of the last remaining

00:02:04.704 --> 00:02:10.877
intact and pristine freshwater ecosystems
in the South Pacific region.

00:02:12.045 --> 00:02:16.257
It is a living memory of my ancestors

00:02:16.299 --> 00:02:20.553
who arrived on that island
50,000 years ago.

00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:23.473
Or more than that.

00:02:24.766 --> 00:02:28.144
We are the Indigenous
guardians of that river,

00:02:28.144 --> 00:02:34.025
and so it is our calling
to protect it from harm

00:02:34.025 --> 00:02:35.652
for our children

00:02:35.694 --> 00:02:38.238
who are yet to arrive from the future.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.785
I went home to be with my people
to protect our livelihood

00:02:43.827 --> 00:02:45.245
and our heritage.

00:02:45.245 --> 00:02:47.455
But here's where the plot twists.

00:02:48.081 --> 00:02:49.290
When I returned home,

00:02:49.332 --> 00:02:52.377
the first thing I did was
I wanted to build myself a house.

00:02:52.419 --> 00:02:55.588
People in the villages understood
that I was away from home

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:56.923
for a long time,

00:02:56.923 --> 00:03:02.512
so they advised me to build a house
that was made of traditional materials,

00:03:02.512 --> 00:03:04.889
using traditional knowledge and skills.

00:03:05.932 --> 00:03:09.144
Many of the houses in my village

00:03:09.144 --> 00:03:13.940
still have roof made
of thatched sago leaves

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.318
held together by cane vines

00:03:17.318 --> 00:03:21.030
and other renewable resources
from the nearby forest.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.909
The designs continue to change over time

00:03:24.909 --> 00:03:27.370
because of climate change,

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:28.997
but I didn’t listen to them.

00:03:29.038 --> 00:03:31.332
You see, I went to a modern school.

00:03:31.374 --> 00:03:33.626
I know more and I know better.

00:03:33.668 --> 00:03:38.298
So I built my house on high steel posts,

00:03:38.339 --> 00:03:41.426
iron roofing and all modern materials.

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:44.679
When my house was finished,
they came and told me,

00:03:44.721 --> 00:03:45.889
"You are wrong."

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.182
(Laughter)

00:03:47.223 --> 00:03:49.309
And funny enough, they were right.

00:03:49.309 --> 00:03:51.060
(Laughter)

00:03:51.603 --> 00:03:55.190
So in 2021, we had unusually strong winds,

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.150
and my house got blown over,

00:03:57.192 --> 00:03:58.359
so it tilted sideways.

00:03:58.359 --> 00:04:00.111
And I lost some roof.

00:04:00.862 --> 00:04:04.824
Most of the houses in the village
only experienced mild effects

00:04:04.824 --> 00:04:06.284
of this wind.

00:04:06.326 --> 00:04:08.703
I was really angry
I didn't listen to them.

00:04:08.703 --> 00:04:09.871
(Laughter)

00:04:10.413 --> 00:04:11.581
So here I was,

00:04:11.623 --> 00:04:13.833
I have to spend more money
and more technology

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:15.919
to get my house back on its post.

00:04:16.377 --> 00:04:18.379
In the same year, at the end of the year,

00:04:18.379 --> 00:04:21.549
I went for a longer holiday
from the town I worked in

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:23.635
to stay in this house that I built.

00:04:24.093 --> 00:04:26.095
During the day, I found it hard to breathe

00:04:26.137 --> 00:04:28.973
because of the heat and the humidity.

00:04:29.265 --> 00:04:33.228
So I found myself spending
more time at my cousin's place

00:04:33.228 --> 00:04:38.775
whose house was built using
traditionally designed materials

00:04:38.817 --> 00:04:41.194
collected from the nearby forest.

00:04:41.236 --> 00:04:45.698
So it had better ventilation,
it was cooler,

00:04:45.698 --> 00:04:47.575
it was sturdier during the wind,

00:04:47.617 --> 00:04:52.539
and it allowed the sound and the smell
from the river, the lake and the forest

00:04:52.539 --> 00:04:53.706
to go through.

00:04:53.748 --> 00:04:55.416
It was really pleasant.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.168
The thing is,

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:01.005
what I know deep down
about sustainable development

00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:05.510
and about the deep wisdom
of my ancestors,

00:05:05.552 --> 00:05:07.512
didn't connect with my head.

00:05:08.721 --> 00:05:10.682
I was doing what most of us do.

00:05:10.723 --> 00:05:14.269
We get educated in the modern
world, modern education,

00:05:14.269 --> 00:05:17.522
and we think that, for example,

00:05:17.564 --> 00:05:23.111
climate change can only be solved
by science and technology,

00:05:23.152 --> 00:05:25.738
or at least science and technology alone.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:26.948
Think about it.

00:05:26.948 --> 00:05:30.243
The world thrived for centuries

00:05:30.285 --> 00:05:33.663
until logging, mining and other extractive

00:05:33.705 --> 00:05:36.708
and destructive development

00:05:36.749 --> 00:05:40.003
ripped resources from the earth.

00:05:40.753 --> 00:05:42.380
And here we are,

00:05:42.422 --> 00:05:45.633
looking at experts who advised on that

00:05:45.633 --> 00:05:50.013
to advise to us about the solutions
for climate change.

00:05:51.973 --> 00:05:56.060
Maybe we need to rethink
about the definition of experts.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:59.772
Maybe the experts
are the Indigenous people

00:05:59.814 --> 00:06:04.402
who've lived lifetimes and centuries
and have protected the earth

00:06:04.444 --> 00:06:07.447
and who can help heal and save it now.

00:06:08.031 --> 00:06:14.078
(Applause)

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:21.336
So I learned the value of my Indigenous
knowledge and traditions the hard way.

00:06:21.336 --> 00:06:26.132
And I'm not just talking about building,
constructing my house, for example.

00:06:27.508 --> 00:06:31.804
We have practices
we're beginning to understand

00:06:31.846 --> 00:06:34.599
that are beneficial to the environment.

00:06:34.641 --> 00:06:35.808
For instance,

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:41.314
in my community, we have sacred places
which people are prohibited to go to.

00:06:41.314 --> 00:06:45.151
Scientists are beginning to find out
that these are essential breeding grounds

00:06:45.193 --> 00:06:48.029
for vital plants and animals.

00:06:48.071 --> 00:06:51.741
We have practices which prohibit
certain groups of people

00:06:51.783 --> 00:06:55.453
to harvest certain plants
and animals at certain times.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:00.041
Scientists are beginning to understand
that this was our ancient way,

00:07:00.041 --> 00:07:04.087
which supported the idea
of managing sustainably

00:07:04.087 --> 00:07:06.589
and harvesting resources sustainably.

00:07:08.424 --> 00:07:10.635
We're also beginning to see

00:07:10.677 --> 00:07:13.554
that, in our world, where we live,

00:07:13.554 --> 00:07:15.640
there is an emphasis on kinship,

00:07:15.682 --> 00:07:17.976
relationship and social capital

00:07:17.976 --> 00:07:21.688
which underpins an equitable

00:07:21.729 --> 00:07:24.816
and connected economy and society.

00:07:25.233 --> 00:07:29.612
Which is also the currency
which my people still use today.

00:07:32.365 --> 00:07:37.662
We have spiritual practices
which understand all of this

00:07:37.704 --> 00:07:42.000
and echoes the connectivity
of all this life.

00:07:43.167 --> 00:07:44.544
I don't want to stand here

00:07:44.585 --> 00:07:47.630
and romanticize
about my cultural practices.

00:07:48.089 --> 00:07:49.340
I want to also acknowledge

00:07:49.382 --> 00:07:53.511
that we’ve got remarkable
wonders of this world created,

00:07:53.511 --> 00:07:56.556
for example, wheel, medicine and internet

00:07:56.556 --> 00:07:58.808
and many more inventions,

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:03.187
that have affected positively
some aspects of our lives.

00:08:03.896 --> 00:08:06.024
However, I still want to believe

00:08:06.024 --> 00:08:09.527
that my Papua New Guinean
cultural traditions

00:08:09.569 --> 00:08:15.116
and the Pacific’s Melanesian
greater worldview,

00:08:15.116 --> 00:08:19.662
based on values
such as kinship, relationships,

00:08:19.662 --> 00:08:25.334
and connectedness of all life
and non-life forms,

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:28.880
could be elevated
for solutions for humanity

00:08:28.921 --> 00:08:30.923
and for the planet to thrive.

00:08:31.257 --> 00:08:34.677
I feel that many cultures
and communities around the world

00:08:34.719 --> 00:08:39.807
are in such a hurry to alienate
themselves from the nature

00:08:39.807 --> 00:08:43.478
and treat everything
as fragmented bits and pieces

00:08:43.519 --> 00:08:46.314
that can or cannot be traded.

00:08:47.065 --> 00:08:49.108
While many Indigenous communities

00:08:49.108 --> 00:08:52.195
continue to hold
and acknowledge the world

00:08:52.236 --> 00:08:55.156
as the complex web of connections

00:08:55.198 --> 00:09:00.286
in which humans, us,
are only a mere part of.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:04.082
We in Papua New Guinea,
for example, continue to honor

00:09:04.082 --> 00:09:09.796
and acknowledge our ancestors
in our present day, in different forms,

00:09:09.837 --> 00:09:13.758
and we want to remain
as the best guardians

00:09:13.758 --> 00:09:17.178
for our world, for other people,

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.429
and our children

00:09:18.471 --> 00:09:21.182
who are yet to arrive from future.

00:09:22.391 --> 00:09:24.519
We make these decisions

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:30.858
because we want to protect
our resources in another form,

00:09:30.858 --> 00:09:33.236
in their natural state,

00:09:33.236 --> 00:09:37.281
so that it can sustain and help us.

00:09:38.074 --> 00:09:41.327
We make this decision
because this is who we are,

00:09:41.369 --> 00:09:44.413
because this has always been
the way we lived.

00:09:45.039 --> 00:09:49.043
So it would be against our traditions,
which I've talked about,

00:09:49.043 --> 00:09:50.920
for a company or a business

00:09:50.962 --> 00:09:53.381
to come in and rip
all the resources out.

00:09:53.381 --> 00:09:54.882
We keep them in the ground

00:09:54.882 --> 00:09:57.927
so that everyone benefits and thrives.

00:09:59.303 --> 00:10:02.557
These traditions that we have
and these philosophies

00:10:02.598 --> 00:10:06.060
have guided us for 50,000 years

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:07.979
of sustainable development

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:10.773
and will help us in the wind of change.

00:10:11.607 --> 00:10:14.152
So here's my request to you.

00:10:14.152 --> 00:10:18.406
Stop thinking of us as underdeveloped,

00:10:18.447 --> 00:10:21.117
uneducated, uncivilized.

00:10:21.909 --> 00:10:26.414
And stop insulting our home,

00:10:26.414 --> 00:10:29.917
which are the rivers,
the forests and the lakes,

00:10:29.959 --> 00:10:34.088
by saying, “these are also
underdeveloped and underutilized.”

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:38.176
In fact, our forests, for example,

00:10:38.217 --> 00:10:41.012
are the most sophisticated,

00:10:41.012 --> 00:10:44.390
efficient and effective
carbon storage facility in the world,

00:10:44.432 --> 00:10:48.019
in which no amount of science
and technology can beat.

00:10:48.978 --> 00:10:53.733
And our ... cultural traditions
are so sophisticated,

00:10:53.733 --> 00:10:57.820
we've been able to use these
to protect our forests

00:10:57.820 --> 00:10:59.488
and our resources.

00:11:00.239 --> 00:11:04.702
In fact, it's rampant consumerism,
greed, imperialism,

00:11:04.744 --> 00:11:07.997
often your way of seeing
the world and seeing us,

00:11:07.997 --> 00:11:09.665
that has hurt the planet.

00:11:09.999 --> 00:11:11.334
Stop doing what I did,

00:11:11.375 --> 00:11:16.380
stop thinking that the modern way
of building our future

00:11:16.422 --> 00:11:17.590
is the best.

00:11:19.258 --> 00:11:21.636
It probably is a problem too.

00:11:22.094 --> 00:11:27.266
Communities closest to the climate crisis

00:11:27.266 --> 00:11:31.312
are also communities
closest to some solutions.

00:11:32.188 --> 00:11:33.564
Learn from us.

00:11:34.106 --> 00:11:36.108
Try to understand,

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:39.362
respect and heed Indigenous guardianship.

00:11:39.695 --> 00:11:43.032
Or else, like me,

00:11:43.032 --> 00:11:45.993
the next great wind will blow us all over.

00:11:46.744 --> 00:11:47.912
Thank you.

00:11:47.954 --> 00:11:53.584
(Cheers and applause)

